WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.200
[intro music]

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.840
Good morning, John. It's
Tuesday, February 6th.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.260
Clean chin!

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.560
I'm really sorry, I didn't

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.620
realize that was going to be

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.620
quite that horrible.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.080
Apparently wax is not
made for facial hair.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.320
I've read

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.000
now online

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.180
some really horrible stories

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.400
about big chunks of skin
getting ripped off.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.080
I had no idea, I'm so sorry.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.560
That looked really horrible.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.660
And also, extremely funny.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.480
Three tries!

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:46.840
To get it

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:47.800
the first time

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.060
that, ugh

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.980
Well, at least those hairs won't
grow back for a while.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:52.880
Or maybe

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:53.780
ever

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.460
since you may have done
some permanent damage.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:57.900
Anyway,

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.520
during our tour of the entire
Southeastern United States,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.240
Katherine and I have run
across several

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:04.460
babies.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.420
And I can't help thinking about your response
to the "How do you feel about babies?" question.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.960
You want an army of babies.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.220
Dozens of babies.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.160
But your only problem is you
don't know where to put babies.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.140
That is NOT my only problem.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.640
I have found the babies to be
rather adorable, though.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:18.760
Katherine - "Look at Uncle Hank"

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:20.860
Katherine - "Look at Uncle Hank"

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.220
[Hank's baby voice] "Uncle Hank." I'm 
Uncle Hank! I think that's adorable!

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.140
Yeah, when I married Katherine,
I became an uncle,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.780
because Katherine's brother has a son.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.780
So, when you have a baby, I won't be able to say,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.300
"Oh, I'm an uncle now."

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.800
'Cause I'm already an uncle.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:35.740
Sorry about that.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.460
Anyway, now that I've
met a few

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.360
I actually

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.140
kind of

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.120
like them.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.240
I've met a very interesting baby
here at Katherine's parents' house.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.700
She's called "Baby Shingy".

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.420
I want you to listen very carefully

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.420
to what Baby Shingy has to say.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:53.300
[string pulling]

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.060
Baby Shingy [garbled] - "Play pat-a-cake?"

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.140
Hank - "Play potty cake?"

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:57.820
[string pulling]

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.520
Baby Shingy [garbled] - "I'm sleepy."

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.780
Hank - "I'm stinky."

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:01.600
[string pulling]

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.200
Baby Shingy [garbled] - "Peek-a-boo!" [giggles]

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.100
Hank - "Chicken poo?" [giggles creepily]

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.200
This is the kind of
baby I want.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.100
You can shake it and
it makes noises,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.340
it's

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.020
scary-looking,

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.540
[rough breathing noise]

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.660
[whispers] Here I come, I'm scary baby.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.080
[grunting noises]

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.040
I am still very excited about the
prospect of you having a baby, though.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.080
So don't take the uncle thing the wrong way.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.640
I'm especially excited about the
prospect of you having babies

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.440
because then mom maybe will stop
bothering me about it so much.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:32.440
So,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.420
how's that going for you?

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.500
I can think of a dozen great places to
put a baby inside of your apartment.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:38.580
It could be fun!

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.920
Like, every day, Sarah comes
home, and you'll say,

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.340
"Find the baby!"

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.860
and she has to search through the entire
apartment trying to find where you hid the baby.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:46.520
Wow,

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.360
having a baby's gonna be a lot of fun!

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.960
I hope you let me know as soon
as you have any news on that front.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.520
Just like the time you called me five or
six minutes after you lost your virginity.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.640
It's really important that you
share stuff like that with me.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:02:59.460
Anyway,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.120
I hope both of us are fertile and that
neither of us ever have to be punished again!

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.820
I'll see you tomorrow.

